A||N||2||bklassy||Help! Distributor trouble part deux||||5||
Z||000000||bklassy||11-15-2001||01:05 PM||||I have a 86 SE v6 that cranks but won't fire. <P>I am pretty confident the external ignition coil is good. I thought yesterday that the pickup coil was bad because it looks so rusty. I couldn't get the connector from the coil to the ignition module out to test the coil. <P>Last night I tried again and managed to get the connector out. The pickup coil tests good. However, My wonderful Haynes manual shows one two-wire connector. I have a seperate connector for each wire and didn't note which wire went where-thanks to Haynes. I have a yellow and a green wire and don't know  which goes to what. Does anyone know which color wire goes to what terminal?<P>Finally, does anyone know why the distributor needs to be removed to change the pickup coil? Doesn't the coil go on top of the distributor shaft and can't it just be removed and replaced on the engine?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Voytek||11-15-2001||03:14 PM||||I don't remember which wire color is which but, if I may make a suggestion, if you're anywhere close to a parts store, get a new pickup coil in your hand and look at the plastic connector on the end and how it locks into place on the ingnition module.  That will help you determine which wire is which.<P>As far as your second question, I've changed my pickup coil in August.  Yes, the distributor must come apart to change the coil.<P>The procedure is quite simple.  BE SURE TO MARK THE LOCATION of the distributor with regards to the engine block and of the shaft that turns (on top) with regards to the rest of the distributor (I used a dap of paint on both).  Otherwise your timing will be off.<P>Just so you know, shops recommend having the timing adjusted after p/u coil replacement.  Apparently every coil is slightly different and you can't get the distributor back into the same spot DEAD-ON.  I've had mine adjusted by a shop (with a strobelight) for $20 bucks CDN.<P>Anyways, once you mark the location, undo the retaining bolt to the engine block at the bottom the distributor shaft.  Pull out the distributor.<P>The rest is quite easy.  Disassemble the thing (remember which washers go where).  There is a 'pin' in the bottom of the shaft (I believe) that is hard to see if the shaft is dirty.  Haynes talks about it.  You need to take a punch (I used a screw) and hammer it out.  Don't lose it.  Once it's out, the disassembly is a breeze.  The new p/u coil should be cheap (I paid $12 CDN) and comes with a new retainer.<P>BTW: the ignition module is easier to remove and test than it is to replace the p/u coil.  I would do that first.  Parts stores can usually test those.<P>Have fun.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||DXR_DAD||11-15-2001||09:57 PM||||Yellow goes to the outer pin, green to the inner pin (closest to shaft).<P>DXR_DAD||||reg||3
